Summary:

The meeting covered two linked topics: contemporary debates about Chinese national/ethnic identity and practical implications of AI for translation and interpretation (T&I) professionals. Discussion included a scholarly reading and a practitioner-focused review of AI tools and market shifts.

On Chinese identity, participants presented prepared remarks about modernizing national identity after Western contact, the Sinocentric historical self-conception, and two core identity elements—connectedness to the nation's fate and duty to ancestral heritage. Students argued the Han have been the dominant assimilative identity and noted that early 20th‑century definitions of "Chinese people" expanded to include non-Han groups such as the Manchu, Mongolians, Hui, and Tibetans. The instructor summarized the paper "Wu Construction of Chinese Non-Chinese," arguing the notion of a fixed, superior Chinese culture is a myth because Chinese culture has continuously amalgamated and reinvented itself; this was briefly related to course reading on the Dai Liku Club.

On AI and translation, the team examined changing employment dynamics: mediocre T&I practitioners risk replacement while highly skilled professionals who integrate AI remain in demand. A Spanish perspective piece by John Hernandez was summarized (via Kimberly) that AI will be used across jobs and professionals must learn to use it as a tool. The group reviewed a Middlebury Institute symposium's eight insights (read aloud), emphasizing role flexibility, experimentation with tools, and that clients will often use AI for first-draft work with humans post-editing. The meeting discussed CAT tools, named popular platforms, and used SmartCat as an example of an AI-enabled marketplace that aggregates major AI providers and charges convenience fees, with a cited baseline cost of about $1,200 per year as a consideration for independent practitioners. The instructor recommended two concrete actions: trial free CAT tool accounts to learn workflows and learn to use AI providers directly (OpenAI and Google) to avoid intermediary costs; a planned hands-on walkthrough was signaled but not completed.


